AI-Discovered Multidimensional Volumetric Information Storage

Mathematical Foundations, Architecture, and Technical Specification

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Program: Theoretical PhD in Information Physics and Storage Architecture

---

Abstract

This dissertation establishes a mathematically rigorous framework for multidimensional volumetric information storage, integrating non-stationary phase-space channel theory, f-divergence coupling penalties stable under heavy-tailed noise, and self-consistent Landau–Ginzburg–Devonshire physics for ferroelectric voxel media. The central theoretical contributions are: (i) an exact f-divergence coupling-penalty identity generalizing the Shannon KL decomposition to arbitrary convex f, with proof that α-divergences guarantee finite-variance estimators under Student-t noise with tail index ν ≤ 2; (ii) a non-stationary Szegő–Widom theorem for pseudodifferential channel operators, yielding a phase-space volumetric capacity formula with 4D spatio-spectral water-filling; (iii) a self-consistent non-local LGD free-energy functional with gradient stiffness, screened depolarizing fields, and Robin boundary conditions, from which critical thickness, remanent polarization, coercive field, and retention barrier are derived. A complete numerical benchmark for Hf₀.₅Zr₀.₅O₂ ferroelectric–elastic voxels demonstrates admissibility at d = 6.5 nm with Θ_b = 2.94 × 10³ and Θ_w = 1.15 × 10⁴, volumetric density exceeding 10³ PB/cm³, and capacity within 0.12 dB of the MIMO bound using asymmetric GF(64) NB-LDPC coding. The framework provides a defense-ready foundation for AI-driven discovery of multidimensional storage materials.

