Part I — Mathematical Foundations

1. Notation and Preliminary Definitions

Let \mathcal{M} denote a physical material system and \mathcal{S} = \{S_1,\dots,S_n\} a set of n physical degrees of freedom (DoF). Each S_i takes values in \mathcal{X}_i \subseteq \mathbb{R}, with joint state space \mathcal{X} = \prod_{i=1}^n \mathcal{X}_i. For a storage volume V \subset \mathbb{R}^3, the information-bearing configuration is a measurable map \Phi : V \to \mathcal{X}.

For a matrix \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{A}^\dagger denotes conjugate transpose, \lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{A}) its largest eigenvalue, \operatorname{tr} the trace, \succeq the positive-semidefinite order, and \kappa(\mathbf{A}) = \|\mathbf{A}\|\,\|\mathbf{A}^{-1}\| the condition number. The Brillouin zone is \mathcal{B} = [-\pi,\pi]^d for lattice dimension d.

2. The Multidimensional Storage Channel

Definition 2.1 (Storage Channel). A memoryless storage channel is a triple \mathfrak{C} = (\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y},W) with W:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to[0,1], W(\cdot\mid x) a probability measure for each x. For n DoFs, the multidimensional channel is \mathfrak{C}_n = (\prod_i \mathcal{X}_i,\ \prod_i \mathcal{Y}_i,\ W_n).

Definition 2.2 (Joint Capacity). The joint capacity of \mathfrak{C}_n is

C_n = \sup_{p_{\mathbf{X}}} I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Y}),

where I(\cdot;\cdot) denotes Shannon mutual information.

Definition 2.3 (Product-Restricted Sum-Rate). For independent inputs p_{\mathbf{X}} = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{X_i} with joint detection, the product-restricted sum-rate is

C_n^{\perp} = \sup_{\prod_i p_{X_i}} I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Y}).

Under this definition C_n \ge C_n^{\perp} holds automatically because the supremum in C_n runs over a superset of product distributions.

Definition 2.4 (Operational Channel Independence Coefficient).

\eta_{\text{ch}} := \frac{C_n}{C_n^{\perp}} \in [1,\infty).

Interpretation: \eta_{\text{ch}} = 1 iff coupling neither helps nor hurts; \eta_{\text{ch}} > 1 iff joint detection over coupled inputs yields capacity gain.

Definition 2.5 (Volumetric Information Density). For a storage volume V,

\rho_I = \frac{C_n}{|V|}\quad \text{(bits per unit volume per read)}.

Read bandwidth B_{\text{read}} converts to rate R_I = B_{\text{read}}\,\rho_I.

3. f-Divergence Coupling Penalty Identity

Let f : (0, \infty) \to \mathbb{R} be a convex function with f(1) = 0. For two probability measures P, Q on \mathcal{Y} with P \ll Q, the f-divergence is

D_f(P \parallel Q) = \int_{\mathcal{Y}} f\left( \frac{dP}{dQ} \right) dQ.

Definition 3.1 (f-Information). For an n-dimensional storage channel \mathfrak{C}_n = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{Y}, W_n) with input \mathbf{X} \sim p_{\mathbf{X}}, joint output distribution p_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y}) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} W_n(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) p_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{x}) d\mathbf{x}, and product marginal reference distribution Q_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y}) = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{Y_i}(y_i), the generalized f-information is

I_f(\mathbf{X} ; \mathbf{Y}) := \mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathbf{X}}} \left[ D_f\left( W_n(\cdot \mid \mathbf{X}) \parallel p_{\mathbf{Y}} \right) \right].

The per-dimension marginal f-information is

I_f(X_i ; Y_i) := \mathbb{E}_{p_{X_i}} \left[ D_f\left( p_{Y_i \mid X_i}(\cdot \mid X_i) \parallel p_{Y_i} \right) \right].

Define the uncoupled product transition law under product inputs p_{\mathbf{X}} = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{X_i}:

W_n^{\perp}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) := \prod_{i=1}^n p_{Y_i \mid X_i}(y_i \mid x_i).

Definition 3.2 (f-Coupling Penalty). For a product input p_{\mathbf{X}} = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{X_i}, the generalized f-coupling penalty is

\Delta C_f(p_{\mathbf{X}}) := \sum_{i=1}^n I_f(X_i ; Y_i) - I_f(\mathbf{X} ; \mathbf{Y}).

Theorem 3.1 (General f-Coupling Penalty Identity). For any memoryless multidimensional channel W_n and product input distribution p_{\mathbf{X}} = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{X_i},

\Delta C_f(p_{\mathbf{X}}) = \mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathbf{X}}} \left[ D_f\left( W_n^{\perp}(\cdot \mid \mathbf{X}) \parallel Q_{\mathbf{Y}} \right) - D_f\left( W_n(\cdot \mid \mathbf{X}) \parallel p_{\mathbf{Y}} \right) \right].

Proof. Expanding the marginal f-information sum under product input p_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{x}) = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{X_i}(x_i):

\sum_{i=1}^n I_f(X_i ; Y_i) = \sum_{i=1}^n \int_{\mathcal{X}_i} p_{X_i}(x_i) \int_{\mathcal{Y}_i} f\left( \frac{p_{Y_i \mid X_i}(y_i \mid x_i)}{p_{Y_i}(y_i)} \right) p_{Y_i}(y_i) \, dy_i \, dx_i.

For any i, integrating over all other variables with respect to \prod_{j \neq i} p_{X_j}(x_j) d x_j \cdot \prod_{j \neq i} p_{Y_j}(y_j) d y_j = 1 allows rewriting the sum as an integral over the joint spaces against Q_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y}) = \prod_{i=1}^n p_{Y_i}(y_i). Define the factorized reference density ratio

R^{\perp}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) := \frac{W_n^{\perp}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x})}{Q_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})} = \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{p_{Y_i \mid X_i}(y_i \mid x_i)}{p_{Y_i}(y_i)}.

Substituting I_f(\mathbf{X} ; \mathbf{Y}) directly yields the stated identity. \square

Corollary 3.1.1 (Fisher Information Local Regime). For weak channel coupling where \left| \frac{W_n(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x})}{p_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})} - 1 \right| \ll 1, every twice-differentiable f-coupling penalty is locally proportional to the Shannon KL coupling penalty:

\Delta C_f(p_{\mathbf{X}}) = \frac{f''(1)}{2} \Delta C_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathbf{X}}) + \mathcal{O}\left( \|W_n - p_{\mathbf{Y}}\|_3^3 \right).

4. Heavy-Tailed Noise Regimes and Stability

In sub-10 nm ferroelectric and multiferroic volumetric storage, domain-wall pinning/unpinning events, avalanche switching, and stress-strain relaxation exhibit heavy-tailed impulsivity. We model non-Gaussian channel noise \mathbf{Z} \in \mathbb{R}^n via a multivariate Student-t distribution with tail index \nu > 0 and scale matrix \boldsymbol{\Sigma}:

p_{\mathbf{Z}}(\mathbf{z}) = \frac{\Gamma\left( \frac{\nu + n}{2} \right)}{\Gamma\left( \frac{\nu}{2} \right) (\nu \pi)^{n/2} |\boldsymbol{\Sigma}|^{1/2}} \left( 1 + \frac{1}{\nu} \mathbf{z}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{z} \right)^{-\frac{\nu + n}{2}}.

As \|\mathbf{z}\| \to \infty, p_{\mathbf{Z}}(\mathbf{z}) \sim \|\mathbf{z}\|^{-(n + \nu)}. For \nu \le 2, the noise covariance matrix \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Z} \mathbf{Z}^\top] diverges.

Theorem 4.1 (Variance Divergence of Shannon Capacity Estimator). Let \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{x} + \mathbf{z} where \mathbf{Z} follows a multivariate Student-t distribution with tail index \nu \le 2. The variance of the sample KL divergence density operator \mathfrak{i}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = \ln \frac{W_n(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x})}{p_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})} is infinite:

\operatorname{Var}_{p_{\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y}}} \left[ \ln \frac{W_n(\mathbf{Y} \mid \mathbf{X})}{p_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{Y})} \right] = \infty.

Proof. For large \|\mathbf{y}\|, the likelihood ratio L(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = \frac{p_{\mathbf{Z}}(\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{x})}{p_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})} satisfies

L(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = 1 + \frac{\nu + n}{\nu} \frac{\mathbf{x}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{y}}{1 + \frac{1}{\nu} \mathbf{y}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{y}} + \mathcal{O}\left( \|\mathbf{y}\|^{-2} \right).

Evaluating the second moment of \ln L(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) and switching to polar coordinates r = \|\mathbf{y}\|:

\int_M^\infty r^{2} \cdot r^{-(n + \nu)} \cdot r^{n-1} \, dr = \int_M^\infty r^{1 - \nu} \, dr = \left[ \frac{r^{2 - \nu}}{2 - \nu} \right]_M^\infty.

For \nu \le 2, the integral diverges. \square

Theorem 4.2 (Uniform Boundedness and Finite Variance under Heavy Tails). Let W_n(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) be a channel subject to heavy-tailed noise \mathbf{Z} \sim t_\nu(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}) with any tail index \nu > 0. For α-divergences with \alpha \in (0, 1), the generating function is

f_\alpha(t) = \frac{t^\alpha - 1}{\alpha(\alpha - 1)}, \qquad f''_\alpha(1) = 1.

Then:

(i) The generalized f_\alpha-information is bounded: 0 \le I_{f_\alpha}(\mathbf{X} ; \mathbf{Y}) \le \frac{1}{\alpha(1 - \alpha)}.

(ii) The sample variance of the f_\alpha-coupling penalty estimator is strictly finite for all \nu > 0: \operatorname{Var}\left[ \Delta \hat{C}_{f_\alpha} \right] < \infty.

Proof. (i) By definition,

D_{f_\alpha}(P \parallel Q) = \frac{1}{\alpha(1 - \alpha)} \left[ 1 - \int_{\mathcal{Y}} (dP)^\alpha (dQ)^{1 - \alpha} \right].

By Hölder's inequality with p = 1/\alpha and q = 1/(1-\alpha):

\int_{\mathcal{Y}} (dP)^\alpha (dQ)^{1 - \alpha} \le \left( \int dP \right)^\alpha \left( \int dQ \right)^{1 - \alpha} = 1.

Since the integral term is non-negative and bounded above by 1, 0 \le D_{f_\alpha}(P \parallel Q) \le \frac{1}{\alpha(1 - \alpha)}. Taking expectations preserves this bound independently of the noise distribution.

(ii) Let \mathfrak{i}_{f_\alpha}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) = f_\alpha\left( \frac{W_n(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x})}{p_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})} \right) be the sample density function. Since 0 \le t^\alpha \le 1 + t,

\left| \mathfrak{i}_{f_\alpha}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) \right| \le \frac{1}{\alpha(1 - \alpha)}.

Because the random variable \mathfrak{i}_{f_\alpha}(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y}) is bounded everywhere, Popoviciu's inequality gives

\operatorname{Var}_{p_{\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y}}} \left[ \mathfrak{i}_{f_\alpha}(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y}) \right] \le \frac{1}{4 \alpha^2 (1 - \alpha)^2} < \infty.

This holds for any \nu > 0, including Cauchy noise (\nu = 1). \square

Theorem 4.3 (Tail Index Continuity). Let \mathbf{Z}_\nu \sim t_\nu and \mathbf{Z}_{\nu'} \sim t_{\nu'} be two heavy-tailed noise regimes with tail indices \nu, \nu' \in (0, \infty). The Hellinger coupling penalty \Delta C_H is Lipschitz continuous in total variation distance:

\left| \Delta C_H^{(t_\nu)}(p_{\mathbf{X}}) - \Delta C_H^{(t_{\nu'})}(p_{\mathbf{X}}) \right| \le 4 \sqrt{2 \, d_{\mathrm{TV}}\left( t_\nu, t_{\nu'} \right)}.

Proof. Using \vert D_H^2(P_1 \parallel Q) - D_H^2(P_2 \parallel Q)\vert \le 2 D_H(P_1 \parallel P_2) and D_H^2(P_1 \parallel P_2) \le 2 d_{\mathrm{TV}}(P_1, P_2), summing over n marginal dimensions yields the bound. \square

5. Non-Stationary Phase-Space Channel Theory

For non-stationary channels arising from spatial aberrations, non-uniform domain-wall dynamics, or spatially varying material properties, the kernel depends independently on source and observation positions:

\mathbf{y}(m) = \sum_{m' \in \Omega} A(m, m') \mathbf{x}(m') + \mathbf{z}(m), \qquad m, m' \in \Omega \subset \mathbb{Z}^d,

where \Omega is a d-dimensional spatial lattice domain with volume |\Omega| < \infty, \mathbf{z} \sim \mathcal{CN}(0, \mathbf{K}_N) is non-stationary additive colored noise, and A(m, m') \in \ell^2(\Omega \times \Omega).

Definition 5.1 (Weyl-Wigner Symbol). The continuous phase-space symbol \sigma_A(r, k) \in L^2(\Omega \times \mathcal{B}) of the non-stationary channel operator \mathcal{A}_\Omega is defined on spatial domain r \in \Omega and Brillouin zone k \in \mathcal{B} by the discrete Wigner-Ville transform:

\sigma_A(r, k) := \sum_{\boldsymbol{\rho} \in \mathbb{Z}^d} A\left(r + \frac{\boldsymbol{\rho}}{2}, r - \frac{\boldsymbol{\rho}}{2}\right) e^{-i k^\top \boldsymbol{\rho}}.

Assumption 5.1 (Symbol Class). \sigma_A(r, k) \in S^0_{1,0}(\Omega \times \mathcal{B}), satisfying

\forall \boldsymbol{\alpha}, \boldsymbol{\beta} \in \mathbb{N}_0^d, \quad \left| \partial_r^{\boldsymbol{\alpha}} \partial_k^{\boldsymbol{\beta}} \sigma_A(r, k) \right| \le C_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}, \boldsymbol{\beta}} (1 + \|k\|)^{-|\boldsymbol{\beta}|}.

Theorem 5.1 (Generalized Szegő-Widom Theorem for Non-Stationary Kernels). Let \mathcal{A} be a pseudodifferential operator with symbol \sigma_A(r, k) \in S^0_{1,0}. Let g : \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R} be continuous on the spectrum of the positive self-adjoint channel energy operator \mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T} := \mathcal{A}_{\Omega_T}^\dagger \mathbf{K}_{N, \Omega_T}^{-1} \mathcal{A}_{\Omega_T}. Then as T \to \infty:

\lim_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{|\Omega_T|} \operatorname{Tr}\left[ g\left( \mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T} \right) \right] = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} g\left( \gamma(r, k) \right) \, dk \, dr,

where

\gamma(r, k) := \frac{|\sigma_A(r, k)|^2}{\sigma_N(r, k)}

is the local phase-space Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio symbol, and \sigma_N(r, k) is the phase-space symbol of the noise covariance operator.

Proof Sketch. The composition of pseudodifferential operators corresponds to the Moyal star-product \star of their symbols:

\sigma_{\mathcal{T}}(r, k) = \sigma_{A^*}(r, k) \star \sigma_N^{-1}(r, k) \star \sigma_A(r, k).

Using f \star g = f g + \frac{i}{2} \{f, g\}_{\mathrm{PB}} + \mathcal{O}(\nabla_r^2 \nabla_k^2), where \{\cdot, \cdot\}_{\mathrm{PB}} is the Poisson bracket:

\sigma_{\mathcal{T}}(r, k) = \frac{|\sigma_A(r, k)|^2}{\sigma_N(r, k)} + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Im}\left\{ \sigma_A^*(r, k), \frac{\sigma_A(r, k)}{\sigma_N(r, k)} \right\}_{\mathrm{PB}} + \mathcal{O}(T^{-2}).

The boundary error from spatial truncation is bounded by

\|\mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T} \mathcal{A} \mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T} - \mathcal{A}_{\Omega_T}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2 \le C \cdot \operatorname{Area}(\partial \Omega_T) \cdot \int_{\mathcal{B}} \|\nabla_k \sigma_A(r, k)\|^2 dk = \mathcal{O}(T^{d-1}).

Dividing by |\Omega_T| = \mathcal{O}(T^d), the boundary contribution vanishes. Applying functional calculus to the diagonalized Moyal principal symbol yields the spatial integral over phase space. \square

Theorem 5.2 (Non-Stationary Volumetric Channel Capacity). The asymptotic information capacity per unit volume of a non-stationary spatial channel subject to an average total power constraint

\frac{1}{|\Omega|} \int_\Omega \int_{\mathcal{B}} P(r, k) \frac{dk}{(2\pi)^d} \, dr \le P_{\text{avg}}

is

c_{\text{vol}} = \frac{1}{2 (2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \log_2 \left( 1 + P^*(r, k) \frac{|\sigma_A(r, k)|^2}{\sigma_N(r, k)} \right) \, dk \, dr.

The optimal power allocation via 4D spatio-spectral water-filling is

P^*(r, k) = \max \left( 0, \, \frac{1}{2 \ln 2 \cdot \mu} - \frac{\sigma_N(r, k)}{|\sigma_A(r, k)|^2} \right),

where \mu > 0 satisfies the global volume constraint.

Theorem 5.3 (Spatial Inhomogeneity Capacity Loss Bound). Let C_{\text{Toeplitz}} be the naive capacity calculated from the spatial average \bar{\sigma}_A(k), and C_{\text{non-stat}} be the true non-stationary capacity under equal power allocation P_0. The capacity mismatch is bounded by the phase-space variance of the symbol:

\Delta C \ge -\frac{\log_2(e)}{2 (2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \frac{P_0}{\sigma_N(k) + P_0 |\bar{\sigma}_A(k)|^2} \operatorname{Var}_{r} \left( \sigma_A(r, k) \right) dk,

where \operatorname{Var}_{r}\left(\sigma_A(r, k)\right) := \frac{1}{|\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \left| \sigma_A(r, k) - \bar{\sigma}_A(k) \right|^2 dr.

Proof. Apply a second-order Taylor expansion to f(z) = \log_2(1 + z) around the spatially averaged SINR \bar{z}(k). The linear term vanishes upon spatial integration. Evaluating the second derivative f''(z) = -\frac{\log_2(e)}{(1 + z)^2} and bounding the denominator by its supremum completes the proof. \square

