Part II — Physical Model and Thermodynamic Feasibility

6. Landauer Reference Values

At T = 300\ \mathrm{K}, k_B T = 4.14\times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{J} and E_L = k_B T\ln 2 = 2.87\times 10^{-21}\ \mathrm{J}.

Mechanism E_W (J/bit) \Theta_{\text{write}} = E_W/k_B T\ln 2
Landauer limit 2.87\times 10^{-21} 1
Atomic manipulation \sim 10^{-19} \sim 35
Ferroelectric switching \sim 10^{-18} \sim 350
Molecular conformational \sim 10^{-19} \sim 35
Optical write (state-of-art) \sim 10^{-12} \sim 3.5\times 10^{8}
DRAM write \sim 10^{-15} \sim 3.5\times 10^{5}

7. n-Dimensional Kramers Retention

Let U(\mathbf{x}) be the n-dimensional potential energy landscape over the state manifold \mathcal{X}. Thermal transitions follow Kramers' escape rate along the minimum-energy path:

\tau_{\text{ret}}^{-1} = \nu_0 \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{\Delta U_{\text{saddle}}}{k_B T}\Bigr),\qquad \Theta_{\text{barrier}} := \frac{\Delta U_{\text{saddle}}}{k_B T},

where \nu_0 \sim 10^{12}–10^{13}\ \mathrm{Hz} is the attempt frequency. For \tau_{\text{ret}} \ge 10 years = 3.15\times 10^8\ \mathrm{s},

\Theta_{\text{barrier}} > \ln(\nu_0 \tau_{\text{ret}}) \approx 47 \text{ for }\nu_0 = 10^{12}\ \mathrm{Hz}.

We adopt \Theta_{\text{barrier}} > 45 as the conservative working threshold.

8. Read-Disturb Budget

Non-destructive readout requires E_{\text{read}} \ll \Delta E_{\text{barrier}}, i.e., \Theta_{\text{read}} := E_{\text{read}}/k_B T \ll \Theta_{\text{barrier}}. We adopt the design margin \Theta_{\text{read}} \le \Theta_{\text{barrier}}/10.

9. Joint Feasibility Region

Because writing must overcome the retention barrier,

E_W \ge \Delta E_{\text{barrier}} + k_B T\ln 2 \iff \Theta_{\text{write}} \ge \Theta_{\text{barrier}} + 1.

The admissible region is

\mathcal{F} = \Bigl\{ (\Theta_w,\Theta_b,\Theta_r) : \Theta_b > 45,\ \Theta_w > \Theta_b + 1,\ \Theta_w < 10^4,\ \Theta_r \le \Theta_b/10 \Bigr\}.

The ceiling \Theta_w < 10^4 is a laboratory engineering bound reflecting current on-chip energy delivery limits at nanosecond write pulses.

10. Non-Local Landau–Ginzburg–Devonshire Free-Energy Functional

For an n-dimensional ferroelectric voxel of volume V = d^3 composed of orthorhombic \mathrm{Hf}_{0.5}\mathrm{Zr}_{0.5}\mathrm{O}_2 (Pca2_1 symmetry), the primary order parameter is the polarization along the polar axis, P_z(\mathbf{r}) \equiv P(\mathbf{r}). The total LGD free-energy functional is

F[P] = \int_V \bigl[ f_{\text{bulk}}(P) + f_{\text{grad}}(\nabla P) + f_{\text{elec}}(P, E_{\text{dep}}) + f_{\text{elastic}}(P, \boldsymbol{\epsilon}) \bigr]\,dV + \int_S f_{\text{surf}}(P)\,dS.

Bulk density (up to sixth order):

f_{\text{bulk}}(P) = \alpha_1 P^2 + \alpha_{11} P^4 + \alpha_{111} P^6,

with \alpha_1 = \alpha_0 (T - T_c) the dielectric stiffness.

Gradient density:

f_{\text{grad}}(\nabla P) = \frac{g}{2}\|\nabla P\|^2.

Depolarizing field with finite screening. Under short-circuit boundary conditions, for electrodes of Thomas–Fermi screening length l_s and interfacial dead layer d_d, \epsilon_d:

E_{\text{dep}}(P) = -\frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0 d \left(1 + \dfrac{\epsilon_r \lambda_{\text{eff}}}{d}\right)} P \approx -\frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0 d} P \quad \text{for } d \gg \epsilon_r \lambda_{\text{eff}},

where \lambda_{\text{eff}} = 2 l_s/\epsilon_e + d_d/\epsilon_d. The depolarizing energy density is

f_{\text{elec}}(P) = -\tfrac{1}{2} E_{\text{dep}} P = \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2 \epsilon_0 d}\,P^2.

Euler–Lagrange equation. \delta F/\delta P = 0 yields

g \nabla^2 P(\mathbf{r}) = 2 \left( \alpha_1 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2 \epsilon_0 d} \right) P(\mathbf{r}) + 4 \alpha_{11} P^3(\mathbf{r}) + 6 \alpha_{111} P^5(\mathbf{r}),

with Robin boundary conditions:

\left[ g \,\hat{\mathbf{n}}\cdot\nabla P + \frac{g}{\delta_b} P \right]_S = 0.

First-mode spatial approximation. Decomposing P(x,y,z) = P_0 \cos(k_x x)\cos(k_y y)\cos(k_z z) on [-d/2,d/2]^3, the fundamental wavevector satisfies k_z \approx \pi/(d + 2\delta_b). Integrating f_{\text{grad}} over V converts gradient cost into an effective bulk stiffness:

\langle f_{\text{grad}} \rangle \approx \frac{3g\pi^2}{2 d^2}P_0^2.

Renormalized stiffness:

\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d) = \alpha_1 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2 \epsilon_0 d} + \frac{3 g \pi^2}{2 d^2}.

Critical voxel size. Ferroelectricity destabilizes (\alpha_{1,\text{eff}} = 0) at

d_c = \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{4 \epsilon_0 |\alpha_1|} \left[ 1 + \sqrt{1 + \frac{24 \epsilon_0^2 |\alpha_1| g \pi^2}{\lambda_{\text{eff}}^2}} \right].

Size-dependent remanent polarization. Solving \partial f_{\text{eff}}/\partial P = 0 for d > d_c:

P_r^2(d) = \frac{-\alpha_{11} + \sqrt{\alpha_{11}^2 - 3 \alpha_{111} \alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d)}}{3 \alpha_{111}}.

In the second-order truncation limit (\alpha_{111} \to 0^+):

P_r(d) = \sqrt{\frac{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d)|}{2\alpha_{11}}}.

Size-dependent coercive field:

E_c(d) = \frac{4}{3\sqrt{6}} \frac{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d)|^{3/2}}{\alpha_{11}^{1/2}}.

Barrier density and total barrier:

\Delta U_b(d) = \frac{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d)|^2}{4\alpha_{11}},

\Delta E_{\text{barrier}}(d) = d^3\,\Delta U_b(d),\qquad \Theta_{\text{barrier}}(d) = \frac{d^3 \Delta U_b(d)}{k_B T}.

Write energy:

E_W(d) = 2 P_r(d)\,E_c(d)\,d^3,\qquad \Theta_{\text{write}}(d) = \frac{2 P_r(d) E_c(d) d^3}{k_B T\ln 2}.

