20. Non-Stationary Domain-Wall Degradation Analysis

For a 2 × 2 HZO cell with spatial degradation from domain-wall pinning, the non-stationary kernel is parameterized by a Gaussian degradation envelope

d(r) = 1 - \eta \exp\left( -\frac{\|r - r_0\|^2}{2 w_d^2} \right), \qquad \eta \in [0, 1),

where \eta measures local polarization suppression. The phase-space energy matrix symbol \boldsymbol{\Gamma}(r, k) \in \mathbb{C}^{2 \times 2} has entries

\Gamma_{11}(r, k) = \frac{|\hat{g}_1(k)|^2 d^2(r)}{\sigma_{N,1}^2(r)} + \frac{|h_{21}|^2 |\hat{g}_{21}(k)|^2 d^2(r)}{\sigma_{N,2}^2},

\Gamma_{22}(r, k) = \frac{|h_{12}|^2 |\hat{g}_{12}(k)|^2 d^2(r)}{\sigma_{N,1}^2(r)} + \frac{|\hat{g}_2(k)|^2 \left[1 - \xi \nabla_r^2 d(r)\right]^2}{\sigma_{N,2}^2}.

Applying the non-stationary Szegő–Widom theorem, the capacity degrades linearly with defect area ratio \frac{2\pi w_d^2}{|\Omega|}:

\Delta C(\eta, \xi) = \frac{\log_2(e)}{(2\pi)^2} \frac{2\pi w_d^2}{|\Omega|} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \sum_{m=1}^2 \frac{P_m^{(0)}(k) \lambda_m^{(0)}(k)}{1 + P_m^{(0)}(k) \lambda_m^{(0)}(k)} \left[ \eta \phi_m(k) + \beta \eta \theta_m(k) \right] dk.

For cell dimensions L = 100\ \mathrm{nm}, |\Omega| = 10^4\ \mathrm{nm}^2, pinning width w_d = 12\ \mathrm{nm}, and severity \eta = 0.8, the capacity loss is 3.81\%. Adaptive 4D space-frequency water-filling recovers +0.85\ \mathrm{dB} effective SNR.

