Appendix B — Non-Stationary Phase-Space Channel Theory: Complete Proofs

B.1 Pseudodifferential Operator Calculus

Definition B.1.1 (Hörmander Symbol Class). A function \sigma(r, k) \in C^\infty(\Omega \times \mathcal{B}) belongs to S^m_{1,0}(\Omega \times \mathcal{B}) if for all multi-indices \boldsymbol{\alpha}, \boldsymbol{\beta},

\left| \partial_r^{\boldsymbol{\alpha}} \partial_k^{\boldsymbol{\beta}} \sigma(r, k) \right| \le C_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}, \boldsymbol{\beta}} (1 + \|k\|)^{m - |\boldsymbol{\beta}|}.

Definition B.1.2 (Weyl Quantization). For a symbol \sigma \in S^m_{1,0}, the associated pseudodifferential operator is

(\mathrm{Op}^W(\sigma) u)(r) = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \int_{\Omega} \sigma\left( \frac{r + r'}{2}, k \right) e^{i k^\top (r - r')} u(r') \, dr' \, dk.

Lemma B.1.1 (Moyal Product). The composition of two pseudodifferential operators corresponds to the Moyal star-product of their symbols:

\sigma_{A \circ B}(r, k) = (\sigma_A \star \sigma_B)(r, k) = \sigma_A(r, k) e^{\frac{i}{2}(\overleftarrow{\partial_r} \cdot \overrightarrow{\partial_k} - \overleftarrow{\partial_k} \cdot \overrightarrow{\partial_r})} \sigma_B(r, k).

The asymptotic expansion is

\sigma_A \star \sigma_B = \sigma_A \sigma_B + \frac{i}{2} \{\sigma_A, \sigma_B\}_{\mathrm{PB}} - \frac{1}{8} \sum_{j,l} \partial_{r_j} \partial_{r_l} \sigma_A \partial_{k_j} \partial_{k_l} \sigma_B + \cdots,

where \{f, g\}_{\mathrm{PB}} = \nabla_r f \cdot \nabla_k g - \nabla_k f \cdot \nabla_r g is the Poisson bracket.

B.2 Proof of Theorem 5.1 (Generalized Szegő–Widom)

Theorem B.2.1 (Restatement of Theorem 5.1). Let \mathcal{A} be a pseudodifferential operator with symbol \sigma_A(r, k) \in S^0_{1,0}. Let g : \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R} be continuous on the spectrum of \mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T} = \mathcal{A}_{\Omega_T}^\dagger \mathbf{K}_{N, \Omega_T}^{-1} \mathcal{A}_{\Omega_T}. Then

\lim_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{|\Omega_T|} \operatorname{Tr}\left[ g\left( \mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T} \right) \right] = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} g\left( \gamma(r, k) \right) \, dk \, dr,

with \gamma(r, k) = |\sigma_A(r, k)|^2 / \sigma_N(r, k).

Proof. We proceed in four steps.

Step 1: Spectral asymptotics for pseudodifferential operators.

For a self-adjoint pseudodifferential operator \mathcal{T} with symbol \sigma_{\mathcal{T}}(r, k) \in S^0_{1,0}, the eigenvalue counting function satisfies

\operatorname{Tr}[g(\mathcal{T})] = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} g(\sigma_{\mathcal{T}}(r, k)) \, dk \, dr + \mathcal{O}(T^{d-1}),

as T \to \infty. This is the standard Weyl law for pseudodifferential operators (see Hörmander 1985, Theorem 29.1.5). The error term \mathcal{O}(T^{d-1}) comes from the boundary \partial \Omega_T.

Step 2: Symbol of the channel energy operator.

The channel energy operator is \mathcal{T} = \mathcal{A}^\dagger \mathbf{K}_N^{-1} \mathcal{A}. Its symbol is given by the Moyal product:

\sigma_{\mathcal{T}} = \sigma_{A^*} \star \sigma_N^{-1} \star \sigma_A.

Using the asymptotic expansion of the Moyal product:

\sigma_{A^*} \star \sigma_N^{-1} = \sigma_{A^*} \sigma_N^{-1} + \frac{i}{2} \{\sigma_{A^*}, \sigma_N^{-1}\}_{\mathrm{PB}} + \mathcal{O}(\nabla_r^2 \nabla_k^2).

Then

\sigma_{\mathcal{T}} = \frac{|\sigma_A|^2}{\sigma_N} + \frac{i}{2} \left( \{\sigma_{A^*}, \sigma_N^{-1}\}_{\mathrm{PB}} \sigma_A + \sigma_{A^*} \{\sigma_N^{-1}, \sigma_A\}_{\mathrm{PB}} \right) + \mathcal{O}(T^{-2}).

The first-order term is purely imaginary (since the Poisson bracket of real functions is real, and i times real is imaginary). For the trace of g(\mathcal{T}), only the real part contributes, so the first-order term vanishes upon integration over phase space (it is a total derivative). Thus

\sigma_{\mathcal{T}}(r, k) = \frac{|\sigma_A(r, k)|^2}{\sigma_N(r, k)} + \mathcal{O}(T^{-2}).

Step 3: Boundary error bound.

The spatial truncation operator \mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T} projects onto \Omega_T. The commutator [\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T}] is supported on the boundary \partial \Omega_T. Its Hilbert–Schmidt norm is bounded by

\|[\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T}]\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2 \le C \cdot \operatorname{Area}(\partial \Omega_T) \cdot \sup_{r \in \partial \Omega_T} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \|\nabla_k \sigma_A(r, k)\|^2 dk = \mathcal{O}(T^{d-1}).

Therefore

\|\mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T} \mathcal{A} \mathcal{P}_{\Omega_T} - \mathcal{A}_{\Omega_T}\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2 = \mathcal{O}(T^{d-1}).

Step 4: Trace limit.

Combining Steps 1–3:

\frac{1}{|\Omega_T|} \operatorname{Tr}[g(\mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T})] = \frac{1}{|\Omega_T|} \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d} \int_{\Omega_T} \int_{\mathcal{B}} g\left( \frac{|\sigma_A(r, k)|^2}{\sigma_N(r, k)} \right) dk \, dr + \mathcal{O}(T^{-1}).

Since |\Omega_T| = T^d |\Omega|, dividing by |\Omega_T| and taking T \to \infty:

\lim_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{|\Omega_T|} \operatorname{Tr}[g(\mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T})] = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} g(\gamma(r, k)) \, dk \, dr.

\square

B.3 Proof of Theorem 5.2 (Non-Stationary Volumetric Capacity)

Theorem B.3.1. The asymptotic information capacity per unit volume of a non-stationary spatial channel subject to

\frac{1}{|\Omega|} \int_\Omega \int_{\mathcal{B}} P(r, k) \frac{dk}{(2\pi)^d} \, dr \le P_{\text{avg}}

is

c_{\text{vol}} = \frac{1}{2 (2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \log_2 \left( 1 + P^*(r, k) \gamma(r, k) \right) \, dk \, dr,

with P^*(r, k) = \max(0, \mu^{-1} - \gamma(r, k)^{-1}).

Proof. The mutual information for a Gaussian channel with non-stationary kernel is

I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Y}) = \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}\left[ \log_2\left( \mathbf{I} + \mathcal{T}_{\Omega_T} \mathbf{K}_{\mathbf{X}} \right) \right],

where \mathbf{K}_{\mathbf{X}} is the input covariance. Under spatial stationarity of the input, \mathbf{K}_{\mathbf{X}} is diagonalized by the same phase-space transform, with symbol P(r, k). Applying Theorem B.2.1 with g(x) = \frac{1}{2} \log_2(1 + P(r, k) x):

\frac{I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Y})}{|\Omega_T|} \to \frac{1}{2 (2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \log_2(1 + P(r, k) \gamma(r, k)) \, dk \, dr.

Maximizing over P(r, k) subject to the power constraint via Lagrange multipliers:

\mathcal{J}[P] = \int_{\Omega} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \left[ \frac{1}{2} \log_2(1 + P(r, k) \gamma(r, k)) - \mu P(r, k) \right] dk \, dr.

Setting \delta \mathcal{J}/\delta P = 0:

\frac{1}{2 \ln 2} \frac{\gamma(r, k)}{1 + P(r, k) \gamma(r, k)} - \mu = 0 \implies P^*(r, k) = \frac{1}{2 \ln 2 \cdot \mu} - \frac{1}{\gamma(r, k)}.

Imposing P^* \ge 0 gives the water-filling solution. \square

B.4 Proof of Theorem 5.3 (Spatial Inhomogeneity Capacity Loss)

Theorem B.4.1. Let C_{\text{Toeplitz}} be the naive capacity from \bar{\sigma}_A(k), and C_{\text{non-stat}} the true capacity under equal power P_0. Then

\Delta C \ge -\frac{\log_2(e)}{2 (2\pi)^d |\Omega|} \int_{\mathcal{B}} \frac{P_0}{\sigma_N(k) + P_0 |\bar{\sigma}_A(k)|^2} \operatorname{Var}_r(\sigma_A(r, k)) \, dk.

Proof. Define z(r, k) = P_0 |\sigma_A(r, k)|^2 / \sigma_N(k) and \bar{z}(k) = P_0 |\bar{\sigma}_A(k)|^2 / \sigma_N(k). Taylor-expand f(z) = \log_2(1 + z) around \bar{z}:

f(z) = f(\bar{z}) + f'(\bar{z})(z - \bar{z}) + \frac{1}{2} f''(\xi)(z - \bar{z})^2.

The linear term vanishes upon spatial integration. The second derivative is

f''(z) = -\frac{\log_2(e)}{(1 + z)^2}.

Therefore

\frac{1}{|\Omega|} \int_\Omega f(z(r, k)) dr = f(\bar{z}(k)) - \frac{\log_2(e)}{2|\Omega|} \int_\Omega \frac{(z(r, k) - \bar{z}(k))^2}{(1 + \xi(r, k))^2} dr.

Bounding the denominator by its maximum over \Omega and integrating over k gives the stated bound. \square

