Appendix C — Landau–Ginzburg–Devonshire Derivations

C.1 Euler–Lagrange Equation

Functional: F[P] = \int_V \left[ \alpha_1 P^2 + \alpha_{11} P^4 + \alpha_{111} P^6 + \frac{g}{2} \|\nabla P\|^2 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2 \epsilon_0 d} P^2 \right] dV + \int_S \frac{g}{2\delta_b} P^2 dS.

Variation: For a variation \delta P,

\delta F = \int_V \left[ 2\alpha_1 P + 4\alpha_{11} P^3 + 6\alpha_{111} P^5 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0 d} P \right] \delta P \, dV + \int_V g \nabla P \cdot \nabla \delta P \, dV + \int_S \frac{g}{\delta_b} P \delta P \, dS.

Integrating by parts:

\int_V g \nabla P \cdot \nabla \delta P \, dV = -\int_V g \nabla^2 P \, \delta P \, dV + \int_S g (\hat{\mathbf{n}} \cdot \nabla P) \delta P \, dS.

Setting \delta F = 0 for all \delta P:

Bulk: g \nabla^2 P = 2\left(\alpha_1 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2\epsilon_0 d}\right) P + 4\alpha_{11} P^3 + 6\alpha_{111} P^5.

Boundary: g \hat{\mathbf{n}} \cdot \nabla P + \frac{g}{\delta_b} P = 0 on S.

C.2 First-Mode Approximation

Assume P(x, y, z) = P_0 \cos(k_x x) \cos(k_y y) \cos(k_z z) on [-d/2, d/2]^3. The Laplacian is

\nabla^2 P = -(k_x^2 + k_y^2 + k_z^2) P.

The boundary condition g \hat{\mathbf{n}} \cdot \nabla P + (g/\delta_b) P = 0 at z = d/2:

-g k_z P_0 \sin(k_z d/2) + \frac{g}{\delta_b} P_0 \cos(k_z d/2) = 0 \implies k_z \tan(k_z d/2) = \frac{1}{\delta_b}.

For small \delta_b, k_z d/2 \approx \pi/2, so k_z \approx \pi/(d + 2\delta_b).

The gradient energy density averaged over V:

\langle f_{\text{grad}} \rangle = \frac{g}{2} (k_x^2 + k_y^2 + k_z^2) P_0^2 \approx \frac{3g\pi^2}{2(d + 2\delta_b)^2} P_0^2 \approx \frac{3g\pi^2}{2d^2} P_0^2.

Adding to the bulk quadratic term:

\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d) = \alpha_1 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2\epsilon_0 d} + \frac{3g\pi^2}{2d^2}.

C.3 Critical Thickness

Setting \alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d_c) = 0:

\alpha_1 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{2\epsilon_0 d_c} + \frac{3g\pi^2}{2d_c^2} = 0.

Multiply by 2d_c^2:

2\alpha_1 d_c^2 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0} d_c + 3g\pi^2 = 0.

Since \alpha_1 < 0, let |\alpha_1| = -\alpha_1:

-2|\alpha_1| d_c^2 + \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0} d_c + 3g\pi^2 = 0.

Solving the quadratic:

d_c = \frac{\frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0} + \sqrt{\left(\frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{\epsilon_0}\right)^2 + 24|\alpha_1| g\pi^2}}{4|\alpha_1|}.

This can be rewritten as

d_c = \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}}{4\epsilon_0 |\alpha_1|} \left[ 1 + \sqrt{1 + \frac{24 \epsilon_0^2 |\alpha_1| g \pi^2}{\lambda_{\text{eff}}^2}} \right].

C.4 Remanent Polarization

For \alpha_{111} = 0, the effective free energy density is

f_{\text{eff}}(P) = \alpha_{1,\text{eff}} P^2 + \alpha_{11} P^4.

Minimizing: \partial f_{\text{eff}}/\partial P = 2\alpha_{1,\text{eff}} P + 4\alpha_{11} P^3 = 0.

Non-zero solution: P_r^2 = -\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}/(2\alpha_{11}) = |\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}|/(2\alpha_{11}).

Thus P_r(d) = \sqrt{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}(d)|/(2\alpha_{11})}.

C.5 Coercive Field

The coercive field is the field at which the barrier vanishes. For the second-order model:

E_c = \frac{4}{3\sqrt{6}} \frac{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}|^{3/2}}{\alpha_{11}^{1/2}}.

Using P_r = \sqrt{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}|/(2\alpha_{11})}:

E_c = \frac{4}{3\sqrt{3}} |\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}| P_r.

C.6 Barrier Height

The barrier density is the difference between the free energy at P = 0 and at the local maximum P_{\max}:

\Delta U_b = f_{\text{eff}}(0) - f_{\text{eff}}(P_{\max}) = \frac{|\alpha_{1,\text{eff}}|^2}{4\alpha_{11}}.

C.7 Numerical Consistency Check

